  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1: Create a social connection",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Set up tenant and local configurations for this lab",
      "description": "## Set up tenant and local configurations for this lab\n\n\n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.tenantConfigure) to configure your Auth0 tenant for this lab. This will register a Regular Web Application named **Lab: Web App** (if it doesn't already exist) in the tenant you authorized with the Auth0 Labs extension. It will also update the Allowed Callback URLs for your application.\n>Wait until you see the message that `resources.xml` has been successfully deploy before continuing to the next step.\n\n    ![resources deployed message](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/a0fun/v2/resources.png) \n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.localConfigure) to configure your local environment. This creates `.env` file under the `src/web-app` directory with the appropriate environment variables and values for integrating with Auth0.\n\n    These include:\n    - `ISSUER_BASE_URL`\n    - `CLIENT_ID`\n    - `CLIENT_SECRET`\n\n1. If you do not see the `.env` file created in `src/web-app`, check the following:\n    - You authorized the Auth0 Labs extension with your Auth0 Tenant in the first step of this lab.\n    \n     > See https://auth0-training.github.io/#im-missing-an-env-file-for-my-application--my-application-doesnt-launch for more troubleshooting details.\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 2,
          "character": 1
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "What you'll do",
      "description": "## Here's what you'll do:  \n\n*   Enable GitHub as a social connection for your Web App\n*   Create a new Auth0 Database\n*   Add new users to the database using bulk migration\n\nA connection is a relationship between Auth0 and a source of users, which may include identity providers (such as Google or LinkedIn), databases, or passwordless authentication methods. Auth0 sits between your application and its sources of users, which adds a level of abstraction so your application is isolated from any changes to and idiosyncrasies of each source's implementation."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Log in to your Auth0 dashboard and preview the current login experience",
      "description": "## Log in to your Auth0 dashboard and preview the current login experience\n\nBefore we enable GitHub as a social connection for our application, let's look at the current login experience.\n  \n1. Log in to your Auth0 Dashboard at https://manage.auth0.com. \n> If you have more than one tenant, make sure you have your default **dev-** tenant selected.  \n    \n1. Go to  **Authentication** > **Authentication Profile**.\n1. Select the **Try** button. You should now see the Universal Login page open in a new window.\n1. Close the Universal Login page once you have seen the current login experience.",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 16,
          "character": 12
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 16,
          "character": 14
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Enable GitHub as a social connection",
      "description": "## Enable GitHub as a social connection\n\nIn this lab, you will walk through how to enable a Social connection as if you had an actual social media developer account.  \n  \nIf you were working in a production environment, before connecting your Auth0 app to a third-party service (in this example, GitHub,) [you would need to have a Developer account](https://auth0.com/docs/connections/social/github) with the third party.  \n  \nHowever, for the lab, you will only enable the GitHub social connection and test the login screen to see the result. You will _not_ configure the GitHub Client ID and Client Secret as part of this lab, [as you would for a production environment](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/dashboard/set-up-social-connections).  \n\n\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **Authentication** > **Social**.\n> You can see any existing social connections for your tenant. Google (google-oauth2) is enabled by default.\n\n1. Select **Create Connection**.\n\n1. In the search field, search for **GitHub** and select it.\n> The next page explains the permissions GitHub will have on your tenant.\n    \n1. Select **Continue**.\n    \n1. On the next page, you can configure your new GitHub Social Connection. Keep all of the default values here, including the **“Leave blank to use Auth0 dev keys”** for **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.\n> If you were working in a production environment, before connecting your Auth0 app to a third-party service (in this example, GitHub,) [you would need to have a Developer account](https://auth0.com/docs/connections/social/github) with the third party, and you would enter in your **Client ID** and **Client Secret** associated with an app on your account.\n        \n1. Select **Create**.\n   \n\nNext, we'll configure which applications can use this connection."
    },
    {
      "title": "Configure your app to use the GitHub social connection",
      "description": "## Configure your app to use the GitHub social connection\n\n 1. You should now see your new social connection named **github** and you should now be on the **Applications** tab of its configuration details.\n\n1.  Select the toggle switch to the right of **Lab: Web App** to toggle it on.  \n\n1. Select the **Settings** tab.\n    > Note the expected warning message here that states:\n    \n    >\"This connection is using Auth0 development keys. Auth0 development keys are intended for testing and are not recommended for Production environments. This connection should be configured with your own keys to enable SSO, federated logout, and other features. [More information about Social Connection developer keys](https://auth0.com/docs/connections/social/devkeys)\".\n\n    > This is OK since we are in a non-production environment for the purpose of this lab."
    },
    {
      "title": "Launch the sample application (Lab: Web App) and test social login",
      "description": "## Launch the sample application (Lab: Web App) and test social login\n\n1. Select [this link](command:workbench.action.debug.start) to launch the application locally.\n    >Wait a moment for the application to launch. You can see the progress in the debug console.\n\n1. Ensure the port for the web app (37500) is set to public. \n    - Click the **PORTS** tab below.\n    - Right click **37500** under the Ports column.\n    - Select **Port Visibility** > **Public**.\n    >Note: You will need to repeat these steps if you restart your Codespace.\n\n    ![](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/auth0/labs/ilt/public-ports.gif)\n\n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.openEndpointByName?[\"Lab: Web App\"]) to open the sample app in your browser.\n\n1. In the sample app, select the login link in the top right.\n    >The application redirects to Auth0 for login.\n\n1. You should see the **Continue with GitHub** button rendered on the Universal Login page. \n\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
